  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Cool weather and periodic showers and thunderstorms \nexpected through Tuesday.\n\nUpper level trough axis passing across eastern New York and into New \nEngland this afternoon, is helping to further mature a surface low \noverhead of northwestern Quebec this afternoon. Meanwhile, within \nthe low levels of the atmosphere, temperatures have cooled to around \n+7 to +8 degrees Celsius, supporting lake effect rain showers to \npersist east of Lake Erie and span across much of the western \nSouthern Tier, through the Genesee Valley and northeast of eastern \nLake Ontario.\n\nHeading into this afternoon, with diurnal instability increasing \nshowers may become stronger and develop into thunderstorms. This \nwill especially be the case from the Finger Lakes eastward as the \nshortwave trough passes overhead.\n\nWith the loss of daytime heating this evening, inland convection \nwill diminish. However, the cool cyclonic flow aloft will support \nlake enhanced showers to persist, especially east of Lake Ontario \nwhere a deeper moisture source will lie.\n\nWinds will shift to the WNW Tuesday, as a ridge of high pressure \nbegins to build in across the eastern Great Lakes. First, lake \neffect showers west of the Lakes will shift south/southeast of the \nlakes heading into Tuesday. Then the introduction of drier air will \nact to diminish the lake effect activity from west to east Tuesday. \n\nKey Message 2...The next potential for showers and thunderstorms \narrive Thursday and last through Friday.\n\nThe next trough will dive southeast across the upper Great Lakes \nWednesday before passing across the eastern Great Lakes Thursday and \ninto New England Friday. Similar to the past few days an occluding \nsurface low will slide east across southern Ontario and into Quebec \nin the later half of the week, supporting a pair of fronts to \ntraverse east across the forecast area. Ahead/along the warm frontal \nand cold frontal boundary passages, expect the potential for \nscattered showers and thunderstorms.",
